crates/pitwall-simhost (the iOS Simulator helper) calls private interfaces of
Apple's CoreSimulator and SimulatorKit frameworks, loaded at run time from the
user's own Xcode and never bundled. Which interfaces to call, and the layout
of the touch message it builds, were learned by reading idb
(https://github.com/facebook/idb, FBSimulatorControl), Copyright (c) Meta
Platforms, Inc. and affiliates, licensed under the MIT License. No idb code is
copied; the helper is written in Rust for Pitwall (docs/spec/device.md lists
each interface).

Device tiles for Android talk to scrcpy-server
(https://github.com/Genymobile/scrcpy), Copyright (C) Genymobile and Romain
Vimeux, licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0. Pitwall doesn't
include it: the user provides the file, which Pitwall uses only when its
SHA-256 matches the published release (docs/spec/device.md). Pitwall's
reader of its stream and writer of its control messages are written from
scrcpy's documented protocol (doc/develop.md and its sources).

